 \noindent Marine heatwaves (MHWs) are traditionally identified from daily Sea Surface Temperature (SST)
records, yet this framework does not transfer directly to higher temporal resolution. At hourly scale,
SST reveals sub-daily variability that is largely smoothed out in daily means, including intermittent
threshold exceedances, short-lived interruptions, diurnal cycling, and nocturnal heat retention. These
features complicate the definition of event continuity and persistence, but also provide an opportunity
to examine the internal structure of marine heat extremes in greater detail. This study presents a methodology for detecting and analysing MHWs at hourly resolution. Events
are identified through a duration--gap framework and subsequently decomposed into individual diurnal
cycles, enabling a taxonomy based on intra-event morphology. This allows MHWs to be characterised not
only by duration and intensity, but also by persistence, cycle-to-cycle evolution, and sub-daily dynamics
that are not accessible from daily data. A sensitivity analysis is used to assess the robustness of the framework by systematically varying
key methodological parameters and evaluating their influence on event identification, continuity, and
internal structure. The methodology is tested using in situ hourly SST observations from the Dragonera
buoy in the Balearic Islands, which provide a high-frequency observational reference for evaluating the
temporal behaviour of detected events. The proposed approach is intended as a zoom-in diagnostic layer rather than a replacement for
standard daily MHW detection: daily methods can first be used to identify relevant or extreme events,
after which selected time windows can be re-analysed at hourly resolution to resolve their internal
evolution. As a next step, the resulting SST-based event catalogue will be combined with high-frequency
coastal air-temperature observations from Cyprus to investigate compound marine--atmospheric heat
extremes and their role in amplifying coastal thermal stress, thereby outlining the conceptual
traits of a so-called ``thermal cage''.
